1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,440
Chess must have an answer.

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:04,880
There is a solution to chess.

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:06,440
It has not been solved yet.

4
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:09,000
Checkers was solved some years ago.

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,600
So anyway, there's an answer to chess.

6
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:11,880
There's an answer to checkers.

7
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,320
I don't remember what it is.

8
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:16,560
Either white always wins or black always wins

9
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:20,120
or there's always a stalemate is the answer.

10
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:22,240
And chess also must have such an answer

11
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:24,040
because chess is a finite game.

12
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:25,960
He's like, can't a game of chess go on forever?

13
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:27,680
No, if you actually go and read the rules,

14
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:29,520
if something hasn't happened for a certain number of time,

15
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:31,200
the game ends in a stalemate.

16
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:34,040
So it actually does have a finite limitation.

17
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:36,440
So essentially, if you can imagine

18
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:40,960
going to every conceivable outcome of chess at the end

19
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:43,360
and then going one step before we know what can't happen

20
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:45,160
and we'll erase that, and one step before that

21
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:46,800
and we can start eliminating things

22
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:49,600
and do that through, again, I think the number

23
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:52,040
of different possibilities exceeds the number of atoms

24
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:53,720
in the universe for chess.

25
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:57,640
But anyway, that's just a question of application.

26
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:00,120
It's nothing to do with math.

27
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:02,720
So chess must have a solution.

28
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:05,760
So there's the case of chess.

29
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:07,360
Now, one that's kind of fun is what's called

30
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:08,800
the centipede game.

31
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:11,640
So this is what you can see in figure 12-6.

32
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:13,600
This is one of the most famous games in economics,

33
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:15,320
the centipede game.

34
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:18,280
So the idea of the centipede game is that

35
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:22,360
either I can go and stop the game right now

36
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:24,000
and keep whatever I have,

37
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,880
or I can go and continue the game.

38
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:31,600
If I continue the game, then our joint resources increase.

39
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:35,640
But the problem is that they don't increase evenly.

40
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:38,680
Instead, I need to have the game go on for two more turns

41
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:40,880
in order to actually profit from the game going on.

42
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:44,920
So if I go and say, fine, let's continue the game,

43
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:48,080
and then my opponent says, sucker, game's over,

44
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:49,560
I've actually lost.

45
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:51,480
But if I say, let's continue the game,

46
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:56,120
and my opponent says, do less, and then I'm better off.

47
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:59,560
Now the key thing in the centipede game is that it is finite,

48
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:01,520
so it has exactly 100 turns.

49
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:03,920
So if the game ends immediately,

50
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:05,520
as soon as the first person starts,

51
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:07,720
says, I don't want to play anymore,

52
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:09,840
you each get a reward of one.

53
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:12,280
If it goes on for the full length of 100 turns,

54
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:13,320
each of you gets 100.

55
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:19,480
So common sense says that the game will go on until the end.

56
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:21,760
Why won't we just cooperate so that instead of getting

57
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:23,840
one each, we get 100 each?

58
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:27,320
But because of the structure of this game,

59
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:28,920
it doesn't work out so nicely.

60
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:33,920
Because as you can see, in the turn right before the last turn,

61
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:37,320
the second to last turn, the first player gets 98,

62
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:40,840
and the second player gets 101 if the game ends there.

63
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:42,400
And it is player two that decides

64
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:44,240
whether the game goes on or not.

65
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:46,640
So if you are one turn away from the end,

66
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:49,080
you can say, well, I can either go on for another turn

67
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:52,440
and get 100, or I can stop it right now and get 101.

68
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:54,880
So the selfish thing to do is to end the game right now

69
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:56,720
and I get 101.

70
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:59,800
But if the opponent knows that I'm gonna think this way,

71
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:02,440
why should he cooperate with me in the second,

72
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:04,720
cooperate me in the, so I said,

73
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:06,840
so that was the first to last turn

74
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:07,920
that you wouldn't want to cooperate.

75
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:10,280
Why should he cooperate with me in the second to last turn?

76
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:12,400
But if that's what I, but then why should I cooperate

77
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:13,840
with the third to last turn?

78
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:14,960
Then why should he cooperate with me

79
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:16,760
in the fourth to last turn?

80
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:17,600
And so on.

81
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:19,960
So it's called a centipede game because it has 100 turns.

82
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:24,960
And it looks like it is in everyone's interest to cooperate.

83
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:28,200
And yet when you pay attention,

84
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,440
it is in the, at the very last turn,

85
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:32,800
the player in charge is incented

86
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:36,120
to end the game prematurely, one turn early.

87
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:37,800
But then the other player, if he knows this,

88
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:40,000
wants to end the game two turns early.

89
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,200
But if I know that he's gonna do that,

90
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:42,760
I want to end it three turns early.

91
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:45,680
If he knows I want to end it four turns early and so on.

92
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:47,920
Right, so if we go and go and start solving this

93
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:51,000
by backwards induction, in that second to last turn,

94
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,720
the second player can either have 100 or 101.

95
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:55,680
So what's he gonna do?

96
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:56,600
Get 101.

97
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:59,280
So we can go and erase that last possibility

98
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:00,840
that'll never happen.

99
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:03,360
Right, and now the first player will say,

100
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:05,680
well I can either go and have 99 or 98.

101
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:07,320
Which one do I prefer?

102
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:12,320
99, so I'll go and erase that part of the centipede.

103
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:13,680
And then he'll go and erase it,

104
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,360
and erase it, and erase it, and erase it.

105
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:18,520
And we erase all the way back to turn one.

106
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:21,080
And then in turn one, the other player just says,

107
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:23,320
well I see where this is going.

108
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:25,800
I refuse to play this game at all.

109
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:27,160
I'm just gonna get one.

110
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:29,000
Because otherwise I'm gonna get zero.

111
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:32,160
Right, so you notice if the game ends in turn one,

112
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:33,000
player one gets one.

113
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,040
But if it ends in turns two, player one gets zero.

114
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:39,760
All right, so here is a use of backwards induction,

115
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:42,840
which yields through an external force of logic

116
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:44,160
a very surprising result.

117
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:47,840
Namely that even though it seems like total common sense

118
00:04:47,840 --> 00:04:49,560
for the two players to work together,

119
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:51,320
they will not work together at all.

120
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:55,560
There's a variant of common sense which says,

121
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:58,560
fine, cooperation will break down towards the end sometime.

122
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:03,200
But backwards induction doesn't say

123
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:05,440
it'll break down towards the end sometime.

124
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:07,640
It says it'll break down immediately.

125
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:10,120
Because people say, what, towards the end sometime?

126
00:05:10,120 --> 00:05:11,280
That's not a time.

127
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:13,240
No, no, no.

128
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:16,640
So it'll definitely have broken down by the last turn.

129
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:18,920
So there's no point cooperating the second to last turn.

130
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:20,640
So no point cooperating in the third to last turn.

131
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:22,800
So no cooperating in the fourth to last turn.

132
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:24,200
All the way back.

133
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:28,000
And the game does what we sometimes call unravel.

134
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:29,520
Game theorists say unraveling.

135
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:31,720
If you ever were a kid and you had a string on your clothes

136
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:34,960
and you started pulling and pulling and pulling and pulling

137
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:36,680
and eventually you had no clothes left,

138
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:37,840
just a pile of thread.

139
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:40,280
I don't think I would have caught that far,

140
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:42,840
but I definitely did ruin a shirt or two.

141
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:44,160
I was laying on my end.

142
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:46,120
And I'm like, oh, it's not my fault.

143
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:47,360
It just happened.

144
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:49,960
Didn't happen, you pulled it.

145
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:52,000
Why didn't you leave it alone?

146
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,560
I don't like leaving things alone.

147
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:55,400
I try to fix them.

148
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:57,440
You see, it's clearly getting worse.

149
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:02,120
There's also the great example of the mosquito bite.

150
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:03,200
How many times have you been told,

151
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:05,960
don't scratch a mosquito bite, it will make it worse?

152
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:09,120
In my experience, this is true always.

153
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:12,880
And yet, how many people scratch mosquito bites?

154
00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:13,960
So tempting.

155
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:16,920
It's like, I can't just sit here suffering, doing nothing.

156
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:19,760
I've got to do something that makes it worse.

157
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:21,560
And I believe this is a metaphor

158
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:23,160
economists really need to push.

159
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:27,080
Look, we all know about mosquito bites

160
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:28,480
and how we make problems worse

161
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:30,920
by trying to solve them, right?

162
00:06:30,920 --> 00:06:32,880
That one's undeniable.

163
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:35,200
Maybe there's other things like that in the world.

164
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:36,880
Maybe there's economic problems,

165
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:39,800
which we amplify by trying to solve them.

166
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:42,080
And if someone says, but I can't live with this problem,

167
00:06:42,080 --> 00:06:44,120
I say, well, can you live with a worse problem?

168
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:46,880
Because that's all you're going to do, is make it worse.

169
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:49,400
But anyway, that's the centipede game.

170
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:51,040
Now, as you might guess, in experiments,

171
00:06:51,040 --> 00:06:54,480
do people actually go and immediately stop cooperating

172
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:56,200
and turn the light on in the centipede game?

173
00:06:56,200 --> 00:06:57,400
They do not.

174
00:06:57,400 --> 00:06:59,480
All right, and in fact, that common sense prediction

175
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:01,080
of people, cooperation will break down

176
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:03,000
somewhere near the end.

177
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,800
That actually is pretty right.

178
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:08,840
So like somewhere between turns 95 and 98,

179
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:11,840
that's about when people stop cooperating.

180
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:13,760
And as to when exactly that is varies,

181
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:14,960
and of course, there are some people who do it

182
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:16,960
to the very bitter end,

183
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:19,320
but like sometime in the 90s

184
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:21,800
is where actual cooperation normally breaks down.

185
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:25,920
So here, this vague intuition about,

186
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:27,040
of course, they'll cooperate,

187
00:07:27,040 --> 00:07:30,320
but it will kind of break down as the end approaches,

188
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:33,000
that turns out to be more true, certainly,

189
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,280
then there will be no cooperation.

190
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:38,120
But in any case, the concept backwards deduction

191
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:40,760
does logically imply that there will be no cooperation

192
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:41,840
at all in this game.

193
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:49,080
All right, so and again, why is that?

194
00:07:49,080 --> 00:07:51,080
Well, we're going to talk about some models

195
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:53,440
that might explain real world behavior.

196
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:55,920
Although, again, the longer I stay in economics,

197
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:58,640
the more I am partial to asking,

198
00:07:58,640 --> 00:08:00,040
well, what would psychologists say about this?

199
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:01,680
Isn't that clearly true?

200
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:04,160
And then we could go and try to model that,

201
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:06,280
or we could just say that they actually

202
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:08,400
have more predictive science in this case.

203
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:11,560
Could it be that psychologists know more than us

204
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:12,920
on a bunch of things?

205
00:08:12,920 --> 00:08:13,960
I don't know why not.

206
00:08:16,680 --> 00:08:17,920
So when I was in your shoes,

207
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:20,120
like the word psychology can never be said

208
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:22,480
without sarcasm, italics.

209
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:25,720
Well, you just want to be a psychologist about this.

210
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:30,160
So you can never even say the word without hostility,

211
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:33,400
but we do need to learn to use the word without hostility

212
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:36,360
because it has so much to teach us.

213
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:38,280
Whenever you're cackling with glee

214
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:41,720
about some psychology experiment failed to replicate,

215
00:08:41,720 --> 00:08:44,520
there's a lot of other stuff that does replicate.

216
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:46,400
So of course, there's a lot of economic theories

217
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:48,520
that haven't even been shown to work once.

218
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:51,880
So I don't know if the CentiB game

219
00:08:51,880 --> 00:08:54,200
has ever been confirmed at all,

220
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:55,560
like the backwards induction version

221
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:56,840
where 100, 100 turn game,

222
00:08:56,840 --> 00:08:58,840
cooperation breaks down immediately.

223
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:00,440
You'd have to really think about how you would

224
00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:01,720
make it break down.

225
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:03,920
Like maybe if you had it played by couples

226
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:05,320
in the middle of a divorce,

227
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:08,920
then maybe they would play this way.

228
00:09:08,920 --> 00:09:11,160
Maybe, even there I don't know.

229
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:16,640
Okay, so questions before we move on?

230
00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:20,000
All right, so now let's move on

231
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:23,280
to pure strategy Nash equilibrium, right?

232
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:25,480
And this is a concept that is only a much more

233
00:09:25,480 --> 00:09:29,160
practically useful than the ones we just done.

234
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:31,400
So you can only go so far

235
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:33,360
with strict dominance type reasoning.

236
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:35,000
Backwards induction seems really awesome

237
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:36,160
when you first hear it,

238
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:37,760
until you realize it only works

239
00:09:37,760 --> 00:09:40,360
for these games of perfect information anyway.

240
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:43,200
All right, so and how many interesting situations

241
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:44,880
have involved perfect information?

242
00:09:46,600 --> 00:09:47,920
I've never found one.

243
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:50,000
All right, so I ran into that little friend

244
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:50,840
who says that something,

245
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:52,760
let's see, I guess they're on Facebook.

246
00:09:52,760 --> 00:09:54,120
There's like, what are the odds

247
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:56,920
that Roe versus Wade gets repealed under Trump?

